  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "One run on five hits and eleven strikeouts is not offense. That's a locked door.",
    "what_this_means": "The game got away in the second and fifth innings. Minnesota scored four runs across those two frames while the Yankees got nothing early and almost nothing late. Ryan Weathers lasted four innings, which meant the bullpen had to cover 4.1 innings on a day game before an evening start. Paul Blackburn, Camilo Doval, Jake Bird, and Angel Chivilli combined to allow no runs and struck out four, which kept the game from getting worse but couldn't fix what had already broken. Weathers gave up six hits in four innings. The lineup answered with five hits and left one runner in scoring position, converting that one chance but missing two others. You don't lose games 1\u20136 by accident. This is what happens when the offense shows up absent and the pitching can't manufacture a second chance.",
    "what_to_watch": "The division does not care about moral victories. Four games back in second place, the Yankees are slipping, and inconsistency is the reason. Not bad luck, not small margins, but repeated failures to produce when the moment arrives. Down 4\u20130 after two innings, they were chasing from the start. The bullpen stayed clean, but clean relief doesn't matter when the starter leaves with four runs already in. This is the cost of a dead offense: even good pitching can only delay the result. They wasted the early window.",
